drm, render: post-blend encode mechanism for linear-light color pipelines

nvidia's plane color pipelines always end in linear light, so the final
encode must move behind blending onto the CRTC GAMMA_LUT (spicy smithay
a6a4be6d/650a3457, niri 96c7e4a7; knowledge only, no code reuse). This
adds the backend mechanism; the scene wiring follows in the next step.

Render side: color_pipeline_retarget_linear strips a recipe's trailing
encode curve and normalizes the plane output to 1.0 at linear_max (the
PQ 125 normalization ahead of the curve is kept, so the plane outputs
linear light in the encode curve's input scale). color_pipeline_encode_lut
builds the matching lut_3x1d encode, sampled from the kernel's inverse
curves, for the existing supplied-transform -> GAMMA_LUT commit path.

Backend side: the supplied-transform gamma path created a fresh blob ID
on every commit, which makes drivers treat every flip as a gamma update
(the churn 0046 fixed for colorop DATA blobs). The committed blob plus a
contents snapshot now is the cache: prepare borrows the committed ID
while contents match, apply transfers ownership and refreshes the
snapshot, rollback never destroys a borrowed ID, and teardown releases
both. Atomic and libliftoff share prepare/apply/rollback, so both commit
paths get stable IDs and atomic GAMMA_LUT updates with the plane state.
The liftoff-fallback baseline restore carries the cached flag.

No public ABI changes; the gamma helpers are independent of the colorop
UAPI and compile without it.--- a/include/backend/drm/drm.h
